"The Fateweavers are real: mortal arcanists, once ordinary humans, who work as one collective."
"Fateweavers live long: the oldest on record lived 970 years, the shortest 116; no average is known."
"Fateweavers share collective memories through a web of communication and act by consensus."
"Fateweavers rebuild their consensus with each replacement or addition, over hundreds of years."
"The majority of Fateweavers support the throne and the Demiurge as checks upon them."
"Proto-Fateweavers arose when the Spire network revealed oblivion and higher dimensions."
"Proto-Fateweavers hid that research, wiped records and memories, and became the first collective."
"Fateweavers use prophecy to deter threats and to foster attitudes and discoveries that keep order."
"For thousands of years Fateweavers have suppressed arcanic knowledge, such as making wysana."
"The Fateweavers have long been tapering Academy enrolment."
"Fateweavers work through the Spire web; each Spire's chained augera links everyone in its sphere."
"The Spire augera are the Fateweavers' lenses to see far and near."
"Fateweavers do not watch every lens at all times; lenses can be smudged or tilted."
"The augera call the Fateweavers the ancient-distant-spiders, who read, stitch and write."
"A wellspring calls Prophecy a symphony and the Fateweavers its composers."
"Fateweavers pull threads of arcana found only in higher dimensions: the strings of Fate."
"Fateweavers work with spools of arcana; spools close or run out; reaching back in time is harder."
"In oblivion a Fateweaver's flare is multi-limbed, like a spider pulling threads."
"Knots are fixed points on a life's string; between knots the string runs smooth and free."
"Knots are truly made in oblivion; the physical world sees only their shadows."
"People make knots when they choose; existing knots pull later choices, and resisting costs."
"One knot can unmake another; each unravelled knot trades some of what is for what may be."
"Knots hold intentions, thoughts, feelings, knowings and not-knowings."
"The Fateweavers' handiwork is left unhidden; a skilled reader can see the threads of a life."
"Glyphs give a prophecy's words their weight; the words are received by nexus points."
"Every person is a nexus point and a channel, linked to others in higher dimensions."
"Links may be emotional, psychological, temporal and more; they need no direct contact."
"A prophecy shifts the influence profile of each nexus point it touches."
"The Spire web and the glyphs give a prophecy a quasi-intelligence bent on fulfilling its words."
"Ambiguous wording lets a prophecy drift; Fateweavers shepherd, prune or cultivate its offshoots."
"A prophecy only bends what is already possible, working through people's own inclinations."
"Cornered, a prophecy may force a choice, and it can make unlikely events such as ambushes happen."
"A prophecy resists being thwarted: notes go missing, recall fails, small accidents widen."
"Prophetic links are a prophecy's appendages; they burrow into the grooves of a person's flare."
"A glyph for prophetic links exists but is secret; merely thinking of it nudges the soul."
"Prophetic links cannot be vented like ambient arcana; they can only be named and excluded by glyph."
"The Spires seed prophetic links into ambient arcana, so all within coverage lie under influence."
"Reading a Prophecy aloud stirs arcana; its links snake out and twine around all who hear it."
"The more aware one is of prophetic links, the tighter they hold."
"A prophecy's words are fixed, but the people they point to, their referents, can change."
"Everett Dundale built silver orbs that shield against minor prophecies, not the major one."
"Everett's orbs all went dead after he made one for his son Caden."
"A major Prophecy exists to make a new Fateweaver when one of them is dying."
"The new Fateweaver is chosen to capture the spirit of the present age."
"Making a new Fateweaver must involve a wild augera."
"The augera name the present Prophecy Creating-Selecting-Chain: it selects a person, then chains him."
"The present Prophecy's Chosen One is Ambrose Hawkins, an orphan first-year at the Academy."
"An old man at Ambrose's orphanage told him of the Prophecy, posing as a talent scout."
"The augera call Ambrose Chosen-Blinded-Jailer; he is meant to renew the augera's chains."
"The augera told Ambrose: an ancient-distant-spider will die, and he will take the spider's place."
"Ambrose keeps the Prophecy on glyphed paper in a small wooden scroll case in his bag."
"The Prophecy reads: \"Five threads will lie within your hands / and be your first across the lands;\""
"It goes on: \"five fates will share your hearth; five hearts will teach you pain;\""
"Then: \"five minds will pave your path; five tongues will curse your name;\""
"It ends: \"and one, brought close, becomes the bones / that form your crown o'er mortal thrones.\""
"The Six-Chained-Foundations are Ambrose and five anchors, his closest friends."
"The five anchors are Devon Ka'atryn, Jerric, Emilia, and the twins Kevan and Lynus."
"The anchors give the new Fateweaver life experience and a unique view, as parents shape a child."
"The Prophecy keeps the five and the Chosen One friends; they may clash but are not meant to kill."
"Caden Dundale was the original anchor, the one brought close; Emilia was brought in to replace him."
"The one brought close is to be transformed into bones, power for the Chosen One's foundation."
"Caden took that transformation in Emilia's place, in oblivion."
"Ambrose and his anchors trapped and broke a wild augera; its core went into Emilia."
"Triss Dundale's minor prophecy, \"This daughter shall be your ruin\", was sent to her father."
"Triss's prophecy set Caden up as the original anchor; it is now subsumed into the major one."
"Everett is to meet ruin through the Prophecy, losing his son and driving himself to his end."
"The Dundales were written in to put a question to the Fateweavers: is it worth it?"
"Reeves and Demiurge Caldwell wiped who the Chosen One is from minds Empire-wide, via the Spires."
"Reeves had spent years preparing that weaving, far longer than the Prophecy had been public."
"Everett Dundale helped with the wipe, and Alina Celwyn worked under Reeves on it."
"Passing knowledge as a knot, a Fateweaver skill, is one part of how the wipe found each memory."
"Reeves is not a Fateweaver, but he and Marcus Kant both have ties to them."
"The Fateweaver who met Caden is a woman: white hair, ice-blue eyes, grey robes, an ageless face."
"That Fateweaver speaks with a strange, almost foreign accent and calls the augera shardlings."
"The Fateweaver froze time, stripped Caden's augera shell, and cut the augera's hold on Ambrose."
"The Fateweaver flung Caden halfway across the Empire to the Academy lake."
"The Fateweavers wiped Caden from his friends' memories; no one was written into his place."
"Caden's friends now credit his deeds to themselves; Kant, Reeves and Everett still remember."
"The Fateweavers had not needed to act so openly in almost a thousand years."
"Caden is the unchosen: free of every prophetic link and unplanned by the Fateweavers."
"The augera name Caden unchosen-sighted-{~?~}; the last part is untranslatable."
"Fateweavers cannot read Caden's threads or control him; they must steer him through others."
"Forcing or reading Caden would likely kill him, so the Fateweavers work around him."
"Near Caden, threads jumble; his presence loosened the braid of Ambrose's fate."
"Unchosen does not mean safe: the anchors are the Prophecy's tools to be turned on the unchosen."
"Caldwell is troubled by \"crown o'er mortal thrones\": no past Prophecy ever spoke of thrones."
"Caldwell fears the Prophecy aims at absolute rule of the Empire, or conquest of other nations."
"The date by which Ambrose's Prophecy is to be fulfilled is unknown."
"Caldwell wants the Prophecy fulfilled on other terms: a Fateweaver who is no jailer."
"Ambrose already grows spider-like limbs in oblivion and has begun reading threads."
"Wellsprings and wild augera lie outside Prophecy; a wellspring awaits a Chosen One like Ambrose."
